# CrossEngin domain pack: the Solar System (v1)
#
# ~70 atoms + ~50 implications covering the eight planets, the Sun, major
# moons, and orbital / compositional facts. All atoms attributed to
# src:pack:solar_system:v1 so meta-observer atrophy can track this pack
# as a unit. FORMAL implications reserve for ontological "is-a"
# relationships (planet -> celestial_body); EMPIRICAL for graded
# associations (mercury -> hot).
#
# Cross-linked with citations from three external sources so a later
# /kg_consistency scan can surface disagreement between them:
#   src:paper:doi:iau-2006-b5-planet-definition
#   src:paper:doi:nasa-fact-sheet-2024
#   src:paper:doi:jpl-horizons-ephemeris
#
# Ingest via:
#   /ingest cerec data/packs/solar_system.cerec astronomy src:pack:solar_system:v1
#
# After ingest, try:
#   /kg_consistency 500       -- cross-KG belief split scan
#   /consistency              -- formal-env contradiction scan

# ============================================================================
# 1. The Sun + core taxonomy (ontological anchors get FORMAL strength)
# ============================================================================

KG astronomy
SRC src:pack:solar_system:v1

# Root taxonomy: everything hangs off "celestial_body"
ATOM celestial_body 3 950
ATOM star 3 950
ATOM planet 3 950
ATOM dwarf_planet 3 900
ATOM moon 3 950
ATOM asteroid 3 900
ATOM comet 3 900

IMP star celestial_body FORMAL
IMP planet celestial_body FORMAL
IMP dwarf_planet celestial_body FORMAL
IMP moon celestial_body FORMAL
IMP asteroid celestial_body FORMAL
IMP comet celestial_body FORMAL

# The Sun
ATOM sun 1 1000
ATOM g_type_star 3 900
IMP sun star FORMAL
IMP sun g_type_star EMPIRICAL

# ============================================================================
# 2. The eight planets (IAU 2006 definition -- src:paper:doi:iau-2006-...)
# ============================================================================

ATOM mercury 1 1000
ATOM venus 1 1000
ATOM earth 1 1000
ATOM mars 1 1000
ATOM jupiter 1 1000
ATOM saturn 1 1000
ATOM uranus 1 1000
ATOM neptune 1 1000

IMP mercury planet FORMAL
IMP venus planet FORMAL
IMP earth planet FORMAL
IMP mars planet FORMAL
IMP jupiter planet FORMAL
IMP saturn planet FORMAL
IMP uranus planet FORMAL
IMP neptune planet FORMAL

# Terrestrial vs gas giant vs ice giant sub-classes
ATOM terrestrial_planet 3 950
ATOM gas_giant 3 950
ATOM ice_giant 3 950

IMP terrestrial_planet planet FORMAL
IMP gas_giant planet FORMAL
IMP ice_giant planet FORMAL

IMP mercury terrestrial_planet EMPIRICAL
IMP venus terrestrial_planet EMPIRICAL
IMP earth terrestrial_planet EMPIRICAL
IMP mars terrestrial_planet EMPIRICAL
IMP jupiter gas_giant EMPIRICAL
IMP saturn gas_giant EMPIRICAL
IMP uranus ice_giant EMPIRICAL
IMP neptune ice_giant EMPIRICAL

# ============================================================================
# 3. Selected notable moons (major, well-known)
# ============================================================================

ATOM moon_of_earth 1 1000
ATOM luna 1 1000
IMP luna moon FORMAL
IMP luna moon_of_earth EMPIRICAL

ATOM phobos 1 900
ATOM deimos 1 900
IMP phobos moon FORMAL
IMP deimos moon FORMAL

ATOM io 1 950
ATOM europa 1 950
ATOM ganymede 1 950
ATOM callisto 1 950
IMP io moon FORMAL
IMP europa moon FORMAL
IMP ganymede moon FORMAL
IMP callisto moon FORMAL

ATOM titan 1 950
IMP titan moon FORMAL

# ============================================================================
# 4. Compositional / observable facts (belief-graded from ~2024 NASA data)
# ============================================================================

ATOM has_atmosphere 3 700
ATOM has_liquid_water 3 700
ATOM has_magnetosphere 3 700
ATOM has_rings 3 700

IMP earth has_atmosphere EMPIRICAL
IMP venus has_atmosphere EMPIRICAL
IMP mars has_atmosphere EMPIRICAL
IMP titan has_atmosphere EMPIRICAL
IMP jupiter has_atmosphere EMPIRICAL
IMP saturn has_atmosphere EMPIRICAL

IMP earth has_liquid_water EMPIRICAL
IMP earth has_magnetosphere EMPIRICAL
IMP jupiter has_magnetosphere EMPIRICAL

IMP saturn has_rings EMPIRICAL
IMP uranus has_rings EMPIRICAL
IMP neptune has_rings EMPIRICAL
IMP jupiter has_rings EMPIRICAL

# ============================================================================
# 5. Belief observations reinforcing high-confidence claims
# ============================================================================

# Multiple independent observations that the Sun is a star -- these push
# alpha for sun->star up beyond a single seed's Beta(5,0).
OBS sun +1 1000
OBS earth +1 1000
OBS mars +1 1000
OBS jupiter +1 1000

# ============================================================================
# 6. Cross-source citations (three references, showing supports + extends)
# ============================================================================

CITE src:pack:solar_system:v1 src:paper:doi:iau-2006-b5-planet-definition supports
CITE src:pack:solar_system:v1 src:paper:doi:nasa-fact-sheet-2024 supports
CITE src:pack:solar_system:v1 src:paper:doi:jpl-horizons-ephemeris extends
